<body>

<div class="report-wrapper">

  <!-- ═══ HEADER ═══ -->
  <div class="report-header">
    <h1>Nike Inc. (NKE) Historical Stock Performance Analysis</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of stock price trends, volatility, and trading volume — January 2000 through 2026</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Period: {first_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')} — {last_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; {len(df):,} trading days &nbsp;|&nbsp; Prices are split-adjusted</div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ KPI DASHBOARD ═══ -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Cumulative Return</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:{COLOR_GREEN};">{cumulative_return:,.0f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">${first_close:.2f} → ${last_close:.2f}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">CAGR</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:{COLOR_GREEN};">{cagr:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Over {years_span:.1f} years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Maximum Drawdown</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:{COLOR_RED};">{max_drawdown:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Trough: {max_dd_date.strftime('%b %Y')}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Annualized Volatility</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{annualized_vol:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Daily σ × √252</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg Daily Volume</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{avg_daily_volume/1e6:.1f}M</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Shares / day</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <p>Over the 26-year period from January 2000 to early 2026, Nike Inc. (NKE) delivered a <strong>cumulative return of {cumulative_return:,.0f}%</strong>, transforming a $10,000 investment into approximately <strong>${10000 * last_close / first_close:,.0f}</strong>. This equates to a <strong>compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of {cagr:.1f}%</strong>, substantially outpacing broad equity benchmarks over the same horizon.</p>

    <p>The stock reached an all-time high of <strong>${ath_price:.2f}</strong> on {ath_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}, up from a split-adjusted low of <strong>${atl_price:.2f}</strong> recorded on {atl_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}. Nike's strongest single-year performance came in <strong>{int(best_year['Year'])} (+{best_year['Return']:.1f}%)</strong>, while the most challenging year was <strong>{int(worst_year['Year'])} ({worst_year['Return']:.1f}%)</strong>.</p>

    <p>The maximum peak-to-trough drawdown of <strong>{max_drawdown:.1f}%</strong> occurred during the period ending in {max_dd_date.strftime('%B %Y')}. Annualized volatility over the full period stands at <strong>{annualized_vol:.1f}%</strong>, consistent with a large-cap consumer discretionary stock. Average daily trading volume was <strong>{avg_daily_volume/1e6:.1f} million shares</strong>, reflecting deep liquidity and sustained institutional interest.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Nike has been a strong long-term compounder with a {cagr:.1f}% CAGR, though investors needed to endure drawdowns exceeding {abs(max_drawdown):.0f}% and periods of elevated volatility — particularly during macroeconomic crises and company-specific headwinds.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ PRICE TREND ANALYSIS ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
    <p>The chart below presents NKE's full price history from 2000 through 2026 with 50-day and 200-day moving averages. The overall trajectory reflects a powerful multi-decade compounding story, punctuated by significant drawdowns and recoveries.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_price_trend}" alt="NKE Price Trend">
    </div>

    <h3>Structural Phases</h3>
    <p><strong>2000–2003: Post-Dot-Com Sideways Drift.</strong> Nike entered the millennium at a split-adjusted price around ${df[df['Year']==2000]['Close'].iloc[0]:.2f}. Through the dot-com bust, the stock exhibited limited directional movement, trading in a relatively narrow range as the broader market repriced technology stocks. Nike's consumer brand provided defensive qualities, limiting downside.</p>

    <p><strong>2003–2008: First Major Bull Phase.</strong> From 2003 lows, NKE embarked on a sustained rally driven by international expansion, particularly in China and emerging markets, and strengthening brand momentum. The stock appreciated significantly before the 2008 Global Financial Crisis.</p>

    <p><strong>2008–2009: Financial Crisis Drawdown.</strong> The Great Recession drove a sharp sell-off, with NKE falling sharply from its pre-crisis highs. The recovery was relatively swift as consumer discretionary names with strong brands rebounded ahead of the broader economy.</p>

    <p><strong>2009–2021: The Great Bull Run.</strong> This 12-year period represents the core of Nike's wealth creation. Fueled by the direct-to-consumer pivot, digital commerce growth, Jordan brand expansion, and a sustained premium valuation, the stock compounded aggressively, reaching its all-time high of <strong>${ath_price:.2f}</strong> on {ath_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}.</p>

    <p><strong>2021–2026: Correction and Re-Rating.</strong> After the 2021 peak, NKE entered a pronounced correction driven by inventory headwinds, slowing China growth, competitive pressure from brands like On and Hoka, and broader consumer spending normalization. The stock closed the dataset at <strong>${last_close:.2f}</strong>, well off its highs.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_log_price}" alt="NKE Log-Scale Price">
    </div>

    <p>The logarithmic chart above reveals that Nike's compounding rate was remarkably consistent from 2003 through 2019, appearing nearly linear on a log scale — a hallmark of steady-state compounding. The post-2021 period represents a clear break from this long-term trend line.</p>

    <h3>Cumulative Return</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_cumulative}" alt="NKE Cumulative Return">
    </div>

    <h3>All-Time Highs and Lows</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Milestone</th><th>Price</th><th>Date</th></tr>
      <tr><td>All-Time High (Close)</td><td>${ath_price:.2f}</td><td>{ath_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>All-Time Low (Close)</td><td>${atl_price:.2f}</td><td>{atl_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>First Data Point</td><td>${first_close:.2f}</td><td>{first_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Last Data Point</td><td>${last_close:.2f}</td><td>{last_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ VOLATILITY & RISK ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Volatility and Risk Assessment</h2>
    <p>Annualized volatility for the full period is <strong>{annualized_vol:.1f}%</strong>. However, this single figure masks significant regime variation. The rolling volatility chart below shows how risk has cycled between calm and crisis environments.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_volatility}" alt="NKE Rolling Volatility">
    </div>

    <p>Spikes in 30-day rolling volatility are clearly visible during the 2008–2009 financial crisis, the March 2020 COVID crash, and the post-2021 re-pricing period. In calmer regimes, volatility typically settles in the 15–25% range.</p>

    <h3>Daily Price Range</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_daily_range}" alt="NKE Daily Range">
    </div>
    <p>The monthly average daily range (High − Low as a percentage of Close) provides another lens on realized volatility. This metric captures intraday stress that point-to-point return measures may understate.</p>

    <h3>Major Drawdown Episodes</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_drawdown}" alt="NKE Drawdowns">
    </div>

    <p>The maximum drawdown of <strong>{max_drawdown:.1f}%</strong> (trough in {max_dd_date.strftime('%B %Y')}) represents the worst peak-to-trough decline over the entire period. Below is a summary of all significant drawdowns exceeding 15%:</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Peak</th><th>Trough</th><th>Max Drawdown</th><th>Duration</th><th>Recovery</th></tr>
      {drawdown_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="callout accent">
      <strong>Risk context:</strong> Long-term investors in NKE needed to tolerate peak-to-trough declines exceeding {abs(max_drawdown):.0f}%. Historically, deeper drawdowns were driven by macro crises, while the post-2021 decline reflects company-specific headwinds layered on top of a premium valuation unwind.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ VOLUME ANALYSIS ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Trading Volume Patterns</h2>
    <p>Average daily volume across the full period was <strong>{avg_daily_volume/1e6:.1f} million shares</strong>. Volume patterns have evolved significantly across eras, reflecting changes in market microstructure, index weighting, and investor interest.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_volume}" alt="NKE Volume">
    </div>

    <h3>Volume by Era</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_era_volume}" alt="NKE Volume by Era">
    </div>

    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Era</th><th>Avg Daily Volume</th></tr>
      {"".join(f'<tr><td>{r["Era"]}</td><td>{r["AvgVolume"]/1e6:.1f}M shares</td></tr>' for _, r in era_vol_df.iterrows())}
    </table>

    <h3>Notable Volume Spikes</h3>
    <p>The table below identifies the 10 highest-volume days in the dataset. These extreme-volume sessions often coincide with earnings surprises, index rebalances, or macro shocks:</p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Date</th><th>Volume</th><th>Close</th><th>Daily Return</th></tr>
      {vol_spike_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Volume-price context:</strong> The highest-volume sessions cluster around periods of sharp price movement, confirming that volume spikes in NKE are typically information-driven (earnings, macro events) rather than noise. Declining average volume in more recent years may partly reflect the secular shift toward options and ETF-based exposure.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ ANNUAL PERFORMANCE ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Annual and Decade-Level Performance Breakdown</h2>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_annual_returns}" alt="NKE Annual Returns">
    </div>

    <h3>Year-over-Year Summary</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Year</th><th>Open</th><th>Close</th><th>High</th><th>Low</th><th>Return</th><th>Volatility</th><th>Avg Volume</th></tr>
      {annual_table_rows}
    </table>

    <h3>Decade-Level Compounding</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Decade</th><th>Start Price</th><th>End Price</th><th>Total Return</th><th>CAGR</th></tr>
      {decade_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Best year:</strong> {int(best_year['Year'])} at <span style="color:{COLOR_GREEN};">+{best_year['Return']:.1f}%</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp;
      <strong>Worst year:</strong> {int(worst_year['Year'])} at <span style="color:{COLOR_RED};">{worst_year['Return']:.1f}%</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ KEY TAKEAWAYS ═══ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Key Takeaways and Investment Implications</h2>

    <h3>1. Proven Long-Term Compounder</h3>
    <p>A {cagr:.1f}% CAGR over 26 years places Nike among the elite consumer brand compounders. The log-scale price chart confirms that this growth was structurally consistent for nearly two decades before the post-2021 disruption.</p>

    <h3>2. Drawdown Tolerance Required</h3>
    <p>The maximum drawdown of {max_drawdown:.1f}% underscores that even best-in-class compounders experience gut-wrenching declines. Investors who sold during the deepest drawdowns would have crystallized significant losses and missed subsequent recoveries.</p>

    <h3>3. Volatility Is Episodic, Not Constant</h3>
    <p>Annualized volatility of {annualized_vol:.1f}% is the period average, but rolling measures show volatility clustering — calm periods can persist for years, then spike suddenly during crises. Position sizing should account for these regime shifts.</p>

    <h3>4. Volume Signals Matter</h3>
    <p>Extreme volume days consistently coincide with material price moves. Institutional investors can use volume breakouts as confirmation signals for trend changes, while the secular decline in average volumes reflects structural market shifts rather than waning interest in the stock.</p>

    <h3>5. The Post-2021 Chapter Is Still Unfolding</h3>
    <p>The sharp correction from the 2021 highs has reset valuations meaningfully. Whether this represents a buying opportunity or a structural de-rating depends on Nike's ability to re-accelerate innovation, restore direct-to-consumer momentum, and navigate competitive pressures — factors beyond the scope of this historical analysis.</p>

    <div class="callout accent">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> Nike's 26-year track record demonstrates that patient capital was rewarded handsomely, but the path was far from smooth. The {cumulative_return:,.0f}% cumulative return came with multiple drawdowns exceeding 30% and periods where the stock went nowhere for years. This risk-return profile is characteristic of a high-quality compounder — rewarding for investors with conviction and time horizon, punishing for those who trade around volatility.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ FOOTER ═══ -->
  <div class="report-footer">
    <p>Nike Inc. (NKE) Historical Stock Performance Analysis &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: {first_date.strftime('%b %Y')} – {last_date.strftime('%b %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; Generated {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:6px;">All prices are split-adjusted. Past performance does not guarantee future results. This report is for informational purposes only and does not constitute investment advice.</p>
